<header><p class="st-kicker">margraviate · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Margraviate of Baden-Baden</h1><p class="st-meta">1535 CE – 1771 CE · capital Baden-Baden; later Rastatt</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Catholic half of the divided margraviate of Baden, ruled from Baden-Baden and Rastatt and made famous by the general 'Türkenlouis'.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The 1535 partition of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/margraviate-of-baden">Margraviate of Baden</a> gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bernhard-iii-margrave-of-baden-baden">Bernhard III</a> the territories around Baden-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden">Baden</a> in the middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> valley, together with a share of the old <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-sponheim">County of Sponheim</a>. His successors resided in the New Castle above Baden-Baden.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>After a Protestant interlude, Baden-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden">Baden</a> was firmly Catholic from the 1620s. Margrave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-william-margrave-of-baden-baden">Louis William</a>, an imperial general known as 'Türkenlouis' for his victories over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>, saw Baden-Baden burned by the French in 1689 and built a new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a> residence at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rastatt">Rastatt</a> from 1700. His widow <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sibylla-augusta-of-saxe-lauenburg">Sibylla Augusta</a> completed it and built the Favorite palace nearby.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>When Margrave August George died without heirs in 1771, his lands passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-frederick-grand-duke-of-baden">Charles Frederick</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/margraviate-of-baden-durlach">Margraviate of Baden-Durlach</a>, reuniting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden">Baden</a>, which became the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/grand-duchy-of-baden">Grand Duchy of Baden</a> in 1806. Baden-Baden later became a fashionable spa town.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/margraviate-of-baden-baden.jpg" alt="Margraviate of Baden-Baden" width="320" height="333"><figcaption>Image: Lencer · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Markgrafschaft_Baden-Baden.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1535 CE – 1771 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>margraviate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Baden-Baden; later Rastatt</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Louis William, Sibylla Augusta, August George</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Alemannic German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Baden-Baden</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1653&amp;focus=margraviate-of-baden-baden" aria-label="Margraviate of Baden-Baden on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4871 475 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Margraviate of Baden-Baden lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5191 731L5191 731"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5191 731h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5191 731h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5191 731h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Baden-Baden. <a href="/atlas?year=1653&amp;focus=margraviate-of-baden-baden">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
